  read='''  <h2>Unfinished business</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>In Lesson 10, freed Texans won rights during Reconstruction, then lost many of them. By 1900, Texas laws kept Black and white Texans apart in schools, trains, and parks, a system called <b>segregation</b>. Black Texans were blocked from voting in Democratic primaries, which in one-party Texas decided most elections. Mexican Americans faced segregated "Mexican schools," juries that left them out, and signs that said they were not welcome.</p>
    <p>Different groups fought back in different ways: in courtrooms, at the ballot box, and in the streets. As you read, compare their <b>goals</b> and their <b>strategies</b>.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Leaders and organizations</h3>
  <div class="regions">
    <div class="card"><div class="ctop"><b>LULAC</b><span class="tag">founded 1929</span></div><p>The League of United Latin American Citizens began in Corpus Christi. It worked through courts and education, backing lawsuits against school segregation such as <i>Delgado v. Bastrop ISD</i> (1948), and <i>Hernandez v. Texas</i> (1954), where the U.S. Supreme Court ruled that Mexican Americans could not be kept off juries.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="ctop"><b>Hector P. García</b><span class="tag">American GI Forum</span></div><p>A doctor and World War II veteran in Corpus Christi. In 1948 he founded the American GI Forum to fight for Mexican American veterans. In 1949 a funeral home in Three Rivers refused a chapel to the family of Felix Longoria, a soldier killed in the war. García's protest led to Longoria's burial at Arlington National Cemetery.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="ctop"><b>Lulu B. White</b><span class="tag">NAACP, Houston</span></div><p>Led the Houston branch of the NAACP in the 1940s and built it into one of the largest in the South. She supported the cases that ended the white primary (<i>Smith v. Allwright</i>, 1944) and opened the University of Texas law school to Heman Sweatt (<i>Sweatt v. Painter</i>, 1950).</p></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="ctop"><b>James Farmer</b><span class="tag">CORE</span></div><p>Born in Marshall and a star debater at Wiley College. He co-founded the Congress of Racial Equality (CORE), which used nonviolent protest, and he organized the 1961 Freedom Rides to challenge segregated buses in the South.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="ctop"><b>Barbara Jordan</b><span class="tag">lawmaker</span></div><p>In 1966 she became the first African American elected to the Texas Senate since 1883. In 1972 she became the first Black woman from the South elected to the U.S. House, where her speech on the Constitution during the Watergate hearings made her nationally known.</p></div>
  </div>

  <h3>Comparing strategies</h3>
  <div class="cmpbox"><table class="cmp">
    <thead><tr><th></th><th>Courts</th><th>Ballot box</th><th>Direct action</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th>How</th><td>Sue to overturn unfair laws</td><td>Register voters, run for office</td><td>Marches, sit-ins, Freedom Rides</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Example</th><td><i>Hernandez v. Texas</i>; <i>Sweatt v. Painter</i></td><td>Barbara Jordan's elections; later, La Raza Unida Party (1970)</td><td>CORE's Freedom Rides</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Strength</th><td>A win changes the law for everyone</td><td>Puts people with a voice in government</td><td>Draws national attention fast</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h3>Political change: Texas turns Republican</h3>
  <div class="prose"><p>For about 100 years after Reconstruction, almost every Texas officeholder was a Democrat. Starting in the 1960s, a conservative movement built the Republican Party in Texas.</p></div>
  <div class="ruler">
    <div class="era"><span class="yr">1961</span><div><b>John Tower</b><span>Won a U.S. Senate seat, the first Republican elected to statewide office in Texas since Reconstruction.</span></div></div>
    <div class="era"><span class="yr">1978</span><div><b>Bill Clements</b><span>Elected the first Republican governor since Reconstruction.</span></div></div>
    <div class="era"><span class="yr">1980s–90s</span><div><b>Realignment</b><span>Many conservative Democrats switched parties. Texans voted for Republicans for president and more state offices.</span></div></div>
    <div class="era"><span class="yr">2002</span><div><b>Full control</b><span>Republicans won majorities in both houses of the Texas Legislature for the first time since Reconstruction.</span></div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout"><b>Structured academic discussion.</b> In a discussion, you listen, build on what others say, and use evidence. Useful sentence starters: "I agree with ___ because ___." "I want to add to what ___ said." "I see it differently, because the evidence shows ___." Talk through today's question with a parent or sibling before you write your claim.</div>''',
